{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11978 - MONDO_18433",
  "verbalization": "Glasdegib is a drug that has been associated with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) in patients with Npm1 somatic mutations or Inv3(p21;q26.2) or T(3;3)(p21;q26.2) translocations. This association is supported by the shared indication with Idarubicin, a chemotherapy agent that is also linked to AML with T(6;9)(p23;q34). The presence of these overlapping indications suggests a potential mechanism of action that could be relevant to the disease. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Glasdegib may interact with pathways that are dysregulated in AML, possibly through mechanisms involving the suppression of tumor cell proliferation or the modulation of immune responses. The shared use of Idarubicin and Aldesleukin further supports a network of interactions that could be relevant to the pathophysiology of AML. While the exact mechanism of Glasdegib's effect remains to be fully elucidated, the integrated evidence suggests that it may act through pathways that are dysregulated in AML, potentially offering a novel therapeutic approach for this disease.",
